  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Phase VI: Global Led Priorities to Carry out Principled, Effective, and Accountable Responses for the Benefit of Crisis-Affected Populations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2022, new and ongoing wars, violence, persecution, and human rights abuses have seen the number of people forcibly displaced reach over 100 million, 71.1 million of whom are internally displaced. IOM now responds to more emergencies than at any other time in its history. Globally, the Department of Operations and Emergencies (DOE) is IOM&#x2019;s institutional lead for emergency response and preparedness, including through global coordination, capacity-building, surge support, as well as policy and strategic guidance. While the global increase in IOM&#x2019;s humanitarian budget and resultant responsibility has led to growth in its field workforce and presence, the Organization&#x2019;s resources for its headquarters-based technical capacity has not expanded at the same rate due to project-based business model and limited core funding, leading to gaps in support, coordination, and coherence.This project contributes to mitigating human suffering by strengthening IOM and partners&#x2019; capacities to address and prepare for mobility dimensions of crisis in a principled, effective, and accountable manner. Building on the last five phases of the project, IOM is consolidating its humanitarian footprint in the following four priority outcome areas: (1) consistent and coherent humanitarian action; (2) data and evidence; (3) capacity building and field support; and (4) strategic partnerships.</narrative>
  </description>
